assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"I finished the Hurco pass and revalidated Okuma and Haas alongside it.\n\nThe calculator now exposes three representative Hurco seats in [calculatorWorkspace.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/web/src/data/calculatorWorkspace.ts#L380), [calculatorWorkspace.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/web/src/data/calculatorWorkspace.ts#L578), and [calculatorWorkspace.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/web/src/data/calculatorWorkspace.ts#L726): `VMX30i`, `VC500i`, and `TM10i`. I also added Hurco-visible page coverage in [CalculatorPage.machineSelection.test.tsx](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/web/src/__tests__/CalculatorPage.machineSelection.test.tsx#L117), Hurco lane/tooling coverage in [calculatorCatalogCoverage.test.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/web/src/__tests__/calculatorCatalogCoverage.test.ts#L1314), deeper Hurco source-catalog assertions in [machineConfigurationOptions.test.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/web/src/__tests__/machineConfigurationOptions.test.ts#L267), and a new backend machinist sanity suite in [hurco-machinist-sanity.test.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/src/__tests__/hurco-machinist-sanity.test.ts#L9).\n\nOkuma and Haas stayed clean in this pass, so no new product fixes were needed for them. Final reruns:\n- Web calculator sweep: `123/123` passing\n- Backend Okuma/Haas/Hurco sweep: `3895/3895` passing\n- Combined total: `4018/4018` passing\n\nOnly remaining noise is non-failing test stderr: the existing Three.js multi-import warning in jsdom and the Vitest `test.poolOptions` deprecation warning."}],"phase":"final_answer"},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"are the physics engines, calculation engines feeding properly into the calculator and making calculations based off all available inputs from a user?\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"Mostly yes for the core calculator solve, but not literally for every possible user input.\n\nFor the main path, the wiring is real end to end:\n[CalculatorPage.tsx](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/web/src/pages/CalculatorPage.tsx#L3896) builds the solve request, posts it via [speedfeed.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/web/src/api/speedfeed.ts), the route forwards it in [speedfeed.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/src/routes/speedfeed.ts#L12), and the dispatcher calls [SpeedFeedOrchestratorEngine.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/src/engines/SpeedFeedOrchestratorEngine.ts#L116) through [calcDispatcher.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/src/tools/dispatchers/calcDispatcher.ts#L6309). That engine is genuinely physics-backed and enriched with torque-curve and tribal-knowledge inputs, not just UI heuristics, as shown in [SpeedFeedOrchestratorEngine.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/src/engines/SpeedFeedOrchestratorEngine.ts#L30) and the integration tests in [physics-pipeline-handbook-integration.test.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/src/__tests__/physics-pipeline-handbook-integration.test.ts#L22) and [tribal-knowledge-consumers.test.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/src/__tests__/tribal-knowledge-consumers.test.ts#L24).\n\nThe important caveat is that the calculator does not pass every UI choice as a first-class engine field. In [calculatorSpeedFeedContract.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/web/src/utils/calculatorSpeedFeedContract.ts#L754), several user inputs get collapsed before the solve:\n- controller capabilities and machine features are reduced to inferred machine dynamics, not sent raw\n- coolant options are reduced to one inferred `coolant_type`\n- workholding preset/category are reduced to coarse `workholding_type` and `workholding_stiffness`\n- `desiredRaUm` and `finishTarget` influence inferred cut type / optimization goal, but the numeric target is not sent as a dedicated standard speed/feed solve target\n- stock shape is mostly reduced to workpiece dimensions\n\nOne bigger limitation remains for nontraditional modes: wire EDM does have its own dedicated solve path in [CalculatorPage.tsx](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/web/src/pages/CalculatorPage.tsx#L3910), but sinker EDM, laser, and waterjet still fall through the generic speed/feed contract. You can see that in [CalculatorPage.tsx](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/web/src/pages/CalculatorPage.tsx#L3953) and in [calculatorSpeedFeedContract.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/web/src/utils/calculatorSpeedFeedContract.ts#L333), where `inferMachineType` returns `undefined` for non-mill, non-lathe modes other than the dedicated wire EDM branch.\n\nSo the honest answer is:\n- `Mill` and `lathe`: yes, properly wired into the real physics/calculation stack\n- `Wire EDM`: yes, through its own dedicated solver path\n- `EDM sinker`, `laser`, `waterjet`: not fully; they still use a generalized path rather than fully mode-specific engine inputs\n- `All available user inputs`: no, not fully; several calculator selections are still inferred/flattened before reaching the backend\n\nIf you want, I can do the next pass to make the standard calculator contract preserve more of the user’s raw selections instead of compressing them."}],"phase":"final_answer"},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"I'm not worried about the sinker, laser or waterjet right now. lets get mill, lathe, wire all working 100%. continue testing every machine manufacturer, tool holder manufacturers and tool holder types, all tooling. don't do a small batch. go all all out and test EVERYTHING\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"Mill, lathe, and wire are tighter now. I fixed a real live-holder wiring bug in [calculatorToolHolderCatalog.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/src/utils/calculatorToolHolderCatalog.ts#L229) and [calculatorToolHolderCatalog.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/src/utils/calculatorToolHolderCatalog.ts#L489): CAPTO turning/boring holders now classify as rigid-turning instead of milling-head-only, CAPTO live-milling holders stay gated behind a milling head, and tool-scoped holder misses no longer spill incompatible generic holders back into the calculator. I also added real-corpus ho