stead of underspecified placeholders.\n\nWhat that means in practice:\n- Applicable taxonomy-native mill and lathe strategies from the mapped CAM systems are now projected into calculator programming environments instead of disappearing behind a smaller fallback list.\n- Wire coverage is broadened around real cut families like `2-axis`, `4-axis taper`, `variable taper`, `rough cut`, `multi-cut skim`, `slug control`, `land/relief`, `rotary wire`, `submerged precision`, and `punch/die`.\n- The calculator now recognizes and routes more native CAM names into the right math families, including things like `drive curve`, `sweeping`, `geodesic`, `equidistant`, `constant cusp`, `rest finishing`, `TrueMill`, `wave roughing`, `thread mill`, `helical bore`, and similar variants.\n- I did not invent brand-new physics engines for every vendor-specific name. For most of these, the correct fix was to map missing native strategies into the existing cutting families that already have the right math. That keeps results accurate instead of duplicating engines for aliases.\n\nCurrent coverage counts from the live backend catalog:\n- `84` programming environments\n- `1,545` total toolpaths\n- `24` mill environments / `893` mill toolpaths\n- `18` lathe environments / `366` lathe toolpaths\n- `17` wire EDM environments / `192` wire EDM toolpaths\n\nVerification is green:\n- Backend programming/data routes: `17/17` passing in `H:\\PRISM\\mcp-server`\n- Web programming/universe/catalog sweeps: `23/23` passing in `H:\\PRISM\\mcp-server\\web`\n- The heavy calculator catalog suite passed, including the max-variability machine sweep and the “every licensed toolpath attached to a legal machine/tool/material lane” proof in [calculatorCatalogCoverage.test.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/web/src/__tests__/calculatorCatalogCoverage.test.ts), [calculatorToolpathUniverseCoverage.test.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/web/src/__tests__/calculatorToolpathUniverseCoverage.test.ts), [calculatorProgrammingCoverage.test.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/web/src/__tests__/calculatorProgrammingCoverage.test.ts), and [calculatorProgrammingCatalog.test.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/src/__tests__/calculatorProgrammingCatalog.test.ts).\n\nOne honest boundary remains: I kept non-cutting/setup-only paths like probing out of this expansion, because the calculator is producing cutting results, not generic CAM workflow metadata. Within the calculator’s real cutting scope for mill, lathe, and wire, the representation is now much closer to complete."}],"phase":"final_answer"},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"give me  think to the app\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"The live app is at [http://localhost:5173](http://localhost:5173).\n\nThe direct calculator page is [http://localhost:5173/calculator](http://localhost:5173/calculator)."}],"phase":"final_answer"},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"we're on the verge of completing the print to program build for lathe and wire is already complete. add missing features and functionality on the calculator page for both machine types. we need an upload drop box for prints/cad prism button for automatic programming with user customizable input and feature selection for both lathe and wire, allow a toggle between 2d/3d part with feature selection and offset input parameters"}]},{"type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"The calculator page now has a real lathe and wire auto-programming lane wired into the backend, not a placeholder surface. I added the shared workbench in [CalculatorProgramWorkbench.tsx](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/web/src/components/calculator/CalculatorProgramWorkbench.tsx), the lathe API seam in [latheTurning.ts](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/web/src/api/latheTurning.ts), and mounted it into [CalculatorPage.tsx](/H:/PRISM/mcp-server/web/src/pages/CalculatorPage.tsx). For both `lathe` and `wire_edm`, the page