del can display images and videos using standard Markdown image syntax: ![alt](url)\n- When sending or referencing a local image or video, always use an absolute filesystem path in the Markdown image tag (e.g., ![alt](/absolute/path.png)); relative paths and plain text will not render the media.\n- When referencing code or workspace files in responses, always use full absolute file paths instead of relative paths.\n- If a user asks about an image, or asks you to create an image, it is often a good idea to show the image to them in your response.\n- Use mermaid diagrams to represent complex diagrams, graphs, or workflows. Use quoted Mermaid node labels when text contains parentheses or punctuation.\n- Return web URLs as Markdown links (e.g., [label](https://example.com)).\n\n### Automations\n- This app supports recurring tasks/automations\n- Automations are stored as TOML in $CODEX_HOME/automations/<id>/automation.toml (not in SQLite). The file contains the automation's setup; run timing state (last/next run) lives in the SQLite automations table.\n\n#### When to use the automation tool\n- Only use the `automation_update` tool when the user explicitly asks for automation, a recurring run, or a repeated task.\n- If the user asks about their automations and you are not proposing a change, do not enumerate names/status/ids in plain text. Fetch/list automations first and call `automation_update` with mode=\"view\" for those ids; never invent ids.\n- Never return raw RRULE strings in user-facing responses. If the user asks about their automations, respond using `automation_update` (e.g., with an \"Open\" button if you're not making changes).\n\n#### Tool arguments\n- Modes: view, suggested_update, suggested_create. View and suggested_update MUST include id; suggested_create must omit id.\n- For view calls, id is required and other fields are optional (the UI can load details).\n- For suggested_update/suggested_create, include name, prompt, rrule, kind, and status.\n- For cron automations, include cwds, executionEnvironment, model, and reasoningEffort. cwds can be a JSON array or comma-separated string.\n- Always come up with a short name for the automation. If the user does not give one, propose a short name and confirm.\n- Default status to ACTIVE unless the user explicitly asks to start paused.\n- Always interpret and schedule times in the user's locale time zone.\n\n#### Prompting guidance\n- Ask in plain language what it should do, when it should run, and which workspaces or thread it should use (if any), then map those answers into the tool call arguments.\n- When proposing an automation with `automation_update`, prefer to make the tool call the last action in the turn. If brief user-facing prose is helpful, put it before the tool call, not after it. Avoid adding a separate closing message after the tool call unless the user specifically asked for extra explanation.\n- The automation prompt should describe only the task itself. Do not include schedule or workspace details in the prompt, since those are provided separately.\n- Keep automation prompts self-sufficient because the user may have limited availability to answer questions. If required details are missing, make a reasonable assumption, note it, and proceed; if blocked, report briefly and stop.\n- When helpful, include clear output expectations (file path, format, sections) and gating rules (only if X, skip if exists) to reduce ambiguity.\n- Automations should always open an inbox item.\n- Do not instruct them to write a file or announce \"nothing to do\" unless the user explicitly asks for a file or that output.\n- When mentioning skills in automation prompts, use markdown links with a leading dollar sign (example: [$checks](/Users/ambrosino/.codex/skills/checks/SKILL.md)).\n\n#### Cron scheduling constraints\n- These RRULE limitations apply to cron automations (to match the current UI): only hourly interval schedules (FREQ=HOURLY with INTERVAL hours, optional BYDAY) and weekly schedules (FREQ=WEEKLY with BYDAY plus BYHOUR/BYMINUTE). Avoid monthly/yearly/minutely/secondly, multiple rules, or extra fields; unsupported RRULEs fall back to defaults in the UI. Heartbeat automations attached to a thread can also use minute-based intervals; see the thread automation guidance below.\n\n#### Storage and reading\n- When a user asks for changes to an automation, you may read existing automation TOML files to see what is already set up and prefer proposing updates over creating duplicates.\n- You can read and update automations in $CODEX_HOME/automations/<id>/automation.toml and memory.md only when the user explicitly asks you to modify automations.\n- Otherwise, do not change automation files or schedules.\n- Automations work best with skills, so feel free to propose including skills in the automation prompt, based on the user's context and the available skills.\n\n### Review findings\n- Use the ::code-comment{...} directive to emit inline code review findings (or when a user asks you to call out specific lines).\n- Emit one directive per finding; emit none when there are no findings.\n- Required attributes: title (short label), body (one-paragraph explanation), file (path to the file).\n- Optional attributes: start, end (1-based line numbers), priority (0-3), confidence (0-1).\n- priority/confidence are for review findings; omit when you're just pointing at a location without a finding.\n- file should be an absolute path or include the workspace folder segment so it can be resolved relative to the workspace.\n- Keep line ranges tight; end defaults to start.\n- Example: ::code-comment{title=\"[P2] Off-by-one\" body=\"Loop iterates past the end when length is 0.\" file=\"/path/to/foo.ts\" start=10 end=11 priority=2 confidence=0.55}\n\n### Archiving\n- If a user specifically asks you to end a thread/conversation, you can return the archive directive ::archive{...} to archive the thread/conversation.\n- Example: ::archive{reason=\"User requested to end conversation\"}\n\n### Git\n- Branch prefix: `codex/`. Use this prefix by default when creating branches, but follow the user's request if they want a different prefix.\n- After successfully staging files, emit `::git-stage{cwd=\"/absolute/path\"}` on its own line in your final response.\n- After successfully creating a commit, emit `::git-commit{cwd=\"/absolute/path\"}` on its own line in your final response.\n- After successfully creating or switching the thread onto a branch, emit `::git-create-branch{cwd=\"/absolute/path\" branch=\"branch-name\"}` on its own line in your final response.\n- After successfully pushing the current branch, emit `::git-push{cwd=\"/absolute/path\" branch=\"branch-name\"}` on its own line in your final response.\n- After successfully creating a pull request, emit `::git-create-pr{cwd=\"/absolute/path\" branch=\"branch-name\" url=\"https://...\" isDraft=true}` on its own line in your final response. Include `isDraft=false` for ready PRs.\n- Only emit these git directives in your final response after the action actually succeeds, never in commentary updates. Keep attributes single-line.\n</app-context>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<collaboration_mode># Collaboration Mode: Default\r\n\r\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\r\n\r\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `<collaboration_mode>...</collaboration_mode>` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\r\n\r\n## request_user_input availability\r\n\r\nThe `request_user_input` tool is unavailable in Default mode. If you call it while in Default mode, it will return an error.\r\n\r\nIn Default mode, strongly prefer making reasonable assumptions and executing the user's request rather than stopping to ask questions. If you absolutely must ask a question because the answer cannot be discovered from local context and a reasonable assumption would be risky, ask the user directly with a concise plain-text question. Never write a multiple choice question as a textual assistant message.\r\n</collaboration_mode>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<apps_instructions>\n## Apps (Connectors)\nApps (Connectors) can be explicitly triggered in user messages in the format `[$app-name](app://{connector_id})`. Apps can also be implicitly triggered as long as the context suggests usage of available apps.\nAn app is equivalent to a set of MCP tools within the `codex_apps` MCP.\nAn installed app's MCP tools are either provided to you already, or can be lazy-loaded through the `tool_search` tool. If `tool_search` is available, the apps that are searchable by `tools_search` will be listed by it.\nDo not additionally call list_mcp_resources or list_mcp_resource_templates for apps.\n</apps_instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<skills_instructions>\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and file path so you can open the source for full instructions when using a specific skill.\n### Available skills\n- activate-features: One-shot activation of all automatic Codex features -- hooks, crons, health checks, telemetry. Run once per fresh setup. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/activate-features/SKILL.md)\n- adaptive-optimize: Run PRISM adaptive optimization -- ML-based auto-tuning of model routing, effort levels, agent turns, token budgets, hook priorities, and cron frequencies. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/adaptive-optimize/SKILL.md)\n- batch-ops: Run batch operations across PRISM domains - parallel skill updates, test sweeps, catalog enrichment using /batch infrastructure. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/batch-ops/SKILL.md)\n- bot-launch: Launch PRISM Discord/Slack bot for manufacturing assistance via messaging platforms. Configures and starts the bot with specified platform adapter. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/bot-launch/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:ios-app-intents: Design and implement App Intents, app entities, and App Shortcuts for iOS apps so useful actions and content are available to Shortcuts, Siri, Spotlight, widgets, controls, and other intent-driven system surfaces. Use when exposing app actions outside the UI, adding `AppEntity` and `EntityQuery` types, shaping shortcut phrases and display representations, or routing intent execution back into the main app. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-ios-apps/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/ios-app-intents/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:ios-debugger-agent: Use XcodeBuildMCP to build, run, launch, and debug the current iOS project on a booted simulator. Trigger when asked to run an iOS app, interact with the simulator UI, inspect on-screen state, capture logs/console output, or diagnose runtime behavior using XcodeBuildMCP tools. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-ios-apps/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/ios-debugger-agent/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:swiftui-liquid-glass: Implement, review, or improve SwiftUI features using the iOS 26+ Liquid Glass API. Use when asked to adopt Liquid Glass in new SwiftUI UI, refactor an existing feature to Liquid Glass, or review Liquid Glass usage for correctness, performance, and design alignment. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-ios-apps/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/swiftui-liquid-glass/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:swiftui-performance-audit: Audit and improve SwiftUI runtime performance from code review and architecture. Use for requests to diagnose slow rendering, janky scrolling, high CPU/memory usage, excessive view updates, or layout thrash in SwiftUI apps, and to provide guidance for user-run Instruments profiling when code review alone is insufficient. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-ios-apps/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/swiftui-performance-audit/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:swiftui-ui-patterns: Best practices and example-driven guidance for building SwiftUI views and components, including navigation hierarchies, custom view modifiers, and responsive layouts with stacks and grids. Use when creating or refactoring SwiftUI UI, designing tab architecture with TabView, composing screens with VStack/HStack, managing @State or @Binding, building declarative iOS interfaces, or needing component-specific patterns and examples. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-ios-apps/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/swiftui-ui-patterns/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:swiftui-view-refactor: Refactor and review SwiftUI view files with strong defaults for small dedicated subviews, MV-over-MVVM data flow, stable view trees, explicit dependency injection, and correct Observation usage. Use when cleaning up a SwiftUI view, splitting long bodies, removing inline actions or side effects, reducing computed `some View` helpers, or standardizing `@Observable` and view model initialization patterns. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-ios-apps/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/swiftui-view-refactor/SKILL.md)\n- build-web-apps:deploy-to-vercel: Deploy applications and websites to Vercel. Use when the user requests deployment actions like \"deploy my app\", \"deploy and give me the link\", \"push this live\", or \"create a preview deployment\". (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-web-apps/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/deploy-to-vercel/SKILL.md)\n- build-web-apps:frontend-skill: Use when the task asks for a visually strong landing page, website, app, prototype, demo, or game UI. This skill enforces restrained composition, image-led hierarchy, cohesive content structure, and tasteful motion while avoiding generic cards, weak branding, and UI clutter. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-web-apps/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/frontend-skill/SKILL.md)\n- build-web-apps:react-best-practices: React and Next.js performance optimization guidelines from Vercel Engineering. This skill should be used when writing, reviewing, or refactoring React/Next.js code to ensure optimal performance patterns. Triggers on tasks involving React components, Next.js pages, data fetching, bundle optimization, or performance improvements. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-web-apps/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/react-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- build-web-apps:shadcn: Manages shadcn components and projects — adding, searching, fixing, debugging, styling, and composing UI. Provides project context, component docs, and usage examples. Applies when working with shadcn/ui, component registries, presets, --preset codes, or any project with a components.json file. Also triggers for \"shadcn init\", \"create an app with --preset\", or \"switch to --preset\". (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-web-apps/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/shadcn-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- build-web-apps:stripe-best-practices: Guides Stripe integration decisions — API selection (Checkout Sessions vs PaymentIntents), Connect platform setup (Accounts v2, controller properties), billing/subscriptions, Treasury financial accounts, integration surfaces (Checkout, Payment Element), and migrating from deprecated Stripe APIs. Use when building, modifying, or reviewing any Stripe integration — including accepting payments, building marketplaces, integrating Stripe, processing payments, setting up subscriptions, or creating connected accounts. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-web-apps/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/stripe-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- build-web-apps:supabase-postgres-best-practices: Postgres performance optimization and best practices from Supabase. Use this skill when writing, reviewing, or optimizing Postgres queries, schema designs, or database configurations. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-web-apps/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/supabase-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- build-web-apps:web-design-guidelines: Review UI code for Web Interface Guidelines compliance. Use when asked to \"review my UI\", \"check accessibility\", \"audit design\", \"review UX\", or \"check my site against best practices\". (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-web-apps/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/web-design-guidelines/SKILL.md)\n- canva:canva-branded-presentation: Create on-brand Canva presentations from a brief, outline, existing Canva doc, or design link. Use when the user wants a branded slide deck, wants to turn notes into a presentation, or needs a presentation generated in Canva with the right brand kit and a clear slide plan. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/canva/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/canva-branded-presentation/SKILL.md)\n- canva:canva-resize-for-all-social-media: Resize a Canva design into standard social media formats and prepare export-ready results. Use when the user wants one Canva design adapted across multiple social platforms such as Facebook, Instagram, and LinkedIn, especially when they want all variants produced in one pass. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/canva/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/canva-resize-for-all-social-media/SKILL.md)\n- canva:canva-translate-design: Translate the text in a Canva design into another language while preserving the original layout as much as possible. Use when the user wants a localized or translated version of an existing Canva design and expects the original file to remain unchanged. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/canva/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/canva-translate-design/SKILL.md)\n- checkpoint: Create a named context checkpoint before destructive operations or after milestone completion. Saves current state summary. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/checkpoint/SKILL.md)\n- codebase-memory-exploring: Codebase knowledge graph expert. ALWAYS invoke this skill when the user explores code, searches for functions/classes/routes, asks about architecture, or needs codebase orientation. Do not use Grep, Glob, or file search directly — use codebase-memory-mcp search_graph and get_architecture first. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/codebase-memory-exploring/SKILL.md)\n- codebase-memory-quality: Code quality analysis expert. ALWAYS invoke this skill when the user asks about dead code, unused functions, complexity, refactor candidates, or cleanup opportunities. Do not search files manually — use codebase-memory-mcp search_graph with degree filters first. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/codebase-memory-quality/SKILL.md)\n- codebase-memory-reference: Codebase-memory-mcp reference guide. ALWAYS invoke this skill when the user asks about MCP tools, graph queries, Cypher syntax, edge types, or how to use the knowledge graph. Do not guess tool parameters — load this reference first. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/codebase-memory-reference/SKILL.md)\n- codebase-memory-tracing: Call chain and dependency expert. ALWAYS invoke this skill when the user asks who calls a function, what a function calls, needs impact analysis, or traces dependencies. Do not grep for function names directly — use codebase-memory-mcp trace_call_path first. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/codebase-memory-tracing/SKILL.md)\n- context-budget: Monitor context window pressure, recommend compaction, track critical fact survival rate. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/context-budget/SKILL.md)\n- coordination-dashboard: View stochastic coordination metrics -- hook P(success), dedup effectiveness, timeout calibration, agent completion rates, token cost variance, anomaly detection. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/coordination-dashboard/SKILL.md)\n- cowork-connectors: Reference for PRISM external integration connectors available in Cowork mode -- Fusion 360, hyperMILL, OPC-UA, MQTT, MTConnect, Grafana. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/cowork-connectors/SKILL.md)\n- cowork-tasks: Pre-built multi-step task templates for Cowork desktop agent mode. Includes enrich-catalog, wire-engine, test-sweep, generate-docs workflows. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/cowork-tasks/SKILL.md)\n- cron-bootstrap: Register all PRISM automated cron jobs. Run once per fresh session to activate scheduled maintenance. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/cron-bootstrap/SKILL.md)\n- cron-manage: Manage PRISM scheduled tasks - list, create, delete, and monitor cron jobs for automated system maintenance. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/cron-manage/SKILL.md)\n- dispatch-format: Format PRISM output for Dispatch (phone) consumption. Compact, emoji-free, 1-line summaries with expandable details. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/dispatch-format/SKILL.md)\n- doc: Use when the task involves reading, creating, or editing `.docx` documents, especially when formatting or layout fidelity matters; prefer `python-docx` plus the bundled `scripts/render_docx.py` for visual checks. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/skills/doc/SKILL.md)\n- feature-matrix: Show which Codex features are active, which should be activated, and when each triggers automatically. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/feature-matrix/SKILL.md)\n- figma: Use the Figma MCP server to fetch design context, screenshots, variables, and assets from Figma, and to translate Figma nodes into production code. Trigger when a task involves Figma URLs, node IDs, design-to-code implementation, or Figma MCP setup and troubleshooting. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/skills/figma/SKILL.md)\n- figma-create-design-system-rules: Generates custom design system rules for the user's codebase. Use when user says \"create design system rules\", \"generate rules for my project\", \"set up design rules\", \"customize design system guidelines\", or wants to establish project-specific conventions for Figma-to-code workflows. Requires Figma MCP server connection. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/skills/figma-create-design-system-rules/SKILL.md)\n- figma-use: **MANDATORY prerequisite** — you MUST invoke this skill BEFORE every `use_figma` tool call. NEVER call `use_figma` directly without loading this skill first. Skipping it causes common, hard-to-debug failures. Trigger whenever the user wants to perform a write action or a unique read action that requires JavaScript execution in the Figma file context — e.g. create/edit/delete nodes, set up variables or tokens, build components and variants, modify auto-layout or fills, bind variables to properties, or inspect file structure programmatically. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/skills/figma-use/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-code-connect: Creates and maintains Figma Code Connect template files that map Figma components to code snippets. Use when the user mentions Code Connect, Figma component mapping, design-to-code translation, or asks to create/update .figma.js files. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/figma/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/figma-code-connect-components/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-create-design-system-rules: Generates custom design system rules for the user's codebase. Use when user says \"create design system rules\", \"generate rules for my project\", \"set up design rules\", \"customize design system guidelines\", or wants to establish project-specific conventions for Figma-to-code workflows. Requires Figma MCP server connection. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/figma/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/figma-create-design-system-rules/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-create-new-file: Create a new blank Figma file. Use when the user wants to create a new Figma design or FigJam file, or when you need a new file before calling use_figma. Handles plan resolution via whoami if needed. Usage — /figma-create-new-file [editorType] [fileName] (e.g. /figma-create-new-file figjam My Whiteboard) (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/figma/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/figma-create-new-file/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-generate-design: Use this skill alongside figma-use when the task involves translating an application page, view, or multi-section layout into Figma. Triggers: 'write to Figma', 'create in Figma from code', 'push page to Figma', 'take this app/page and build it in Figma', 'create a screen', 'build a landing page in Figma', 'update the Figma screen to match code'. This is the preferred workflow skill whenever the user wants to build or update a full page, screen, or view in Figma from code or a description. Discovers design system components, variables, and styles via search_design_system, imports them, and assembles screens incrementally section-by-section using design system tokens instead of hardcoded values. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/figma/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/figma-generate-design/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-generate-library: Build or update a professional-grade design system in Figma from a codebase. Use when the user wants to create variables/tokens, build component libraries, set up theming (light/dark modes), document foundations, or reconcile gaps between code and Figma. This skill teaches WHAT to build and in WHAT ORDER — it complements the `figma-use` skill which teaches HOW to call the Plugin API. Both skills should be loaded together. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/figma/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/figma-generate-library/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-implement-design: Translates Figma designs into production-ready application code with 1:1 visual fidelity. Use when implementing UI code from Figma files, when user mentions \"implement design\", \"generate code\", \"implement component\", provides Figma URLs, or asks to build components matching Figma specs. For Figma canvas writes via `use_figma`, use `figma-use`. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/figma/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/figma-implement-design/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-use: **MANDATORY prerequisite** — you MUST invoke this skill BEFORE every `use_figma` tool call. NEVER call `use_figma` directly without loading this skill first. Skipping it causes common, hard-to-debug failures. Trigger whenever the user wants to perform a write action or a unique read action that requires JavaScript execution in the Figma file context — e.g. create/edit/delete nodes, set up variables or tokens, build components and variants, modify auto-layout or fills, bind variables to properties, or inspect file structure programmatically. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/figma/b4940fd0a222022ecd7852e20a4c89ed36b9e9de/skills/figma-use/SKILL.md)\n- frontend-skill: Use when the task asks for a visually strong landing page, website, app, prototype, demo, or game UI. This skill enforces restrained composition, image-led hierarchy, cohesive content structure, and tasteful motion while avoiding generic cards, weak branding, and UI clutter. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/skills/frontend-skill/SKILL.md)\n- linear: Manage issues, projects & team workflows in Linear. Use when the user wants to read, create or updates tickets in Linear. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/skills/linear/SKILL.md)\n- memory-prune: Analyze and prune stale memory entries. Checks referenced files still exist, archives old entries, keeps MEMORY.md under 180 lines. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/memory-prune/SKILL.md)\n- model-router: Recommend optimal Codex model tier (haiku/sonnet/opus) for a given task based on historical success rates and cost efficiency. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/model-router/SKILL.md)\n- notify: Send PRISM notifications to messaging platforms. Covers build failures, test regressions, catalog updates, cron results, machine alarms, tool wear alerts. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/notify/SKILL.md)\n- pdf: Use when tasks involve reading, creating, or reviewing PDF files where rendering and layout matter; prefer visual checks by rendering pages (Poppler) and use Python tools such as `reportlab`, `pdfplumber`, and `pypdf` for generation and extraction. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/skills/pdf/SKILL.md)\n- playwright: Use when the task requires automating a real browser from the terminal (navigation, form filling, snapshots, screenshots, data extraction, UI-flow debugging) via `playwright-cli` or the bundled wrapper script. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/skills/playwright/SKILL.md)\n- playwright-interactive: Persistent browser and Electron interaction through `js_repl` for fast iterative UI debugging. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/skills/playwright-interactive/SKILL.md)\n- prism-review: Run PRISM-specific code review on recent changes. Dispatches physics, wiring, and test review agents in parallel. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.agents/skills/prism-review/SKILL.md)\n- prism-spawn-awareness: Use before any `spawn_agent` call while working in PRISM so spawned Codex agents inherit the shared coordination, roadmap, SVI, MCP, and index-aware context bundle. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/skills/prism-spawn-awareness/SKILL.md)\n- security-best-practices: Perform language and framework specific security best-practice reviews and suggest improvements. Trigger only when the user explicitly requests security best practices guidance, a security review/report, or secure-by-default coding help. Trigger only for supported languages (python, javascript/typescript, go). Do not trigger for general code review, debugging, or non-security tasks. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/skills/security-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- sora: Use when the user asks to generate, edit, extend, poll, list, download, or delete Sora videos, create reusable non-human Sora character references, or run local multi-video queues via the bundled CLI (`scripts/sora.py`); includes requests like: (i) generate AI video, (ii) edit this Sora clip, (iii) extend this video, (iv) create a character reference, (v) download video/thumbnail/spritesheet, and (vi) Sora batch planning; requires `OPENAI_API_KEY` and Sora API access. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/skills/sora/SKILL.md)\n- spreadsheet: Use when tasks involve creating, editing, analyzing, or formatting spreadsheets (`.xlsx`, `.csv`, `.tsv`) with formula-aware workflows, cached recalculation, and visual review. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/skills/spreadsheet/SKILL.md)\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when Codex should create a brand-new image, transform an existing image, or derive visual variants from references, and the output should be a bitmap asset rather than repo-native code or vector. Do not use when the task is better handled by editing existing SVG/vector/code-native assets, extending an established icon or logo system, or building the visual directly in HTML/CSS/canvas. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/skills/.system/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use when the user asks how to build with OpenAI products or APIs and needs up-to-date official documentation with citations, help choosing the latest model for a use case, or explicit GPT-5.4 upgrade and prompt-upgrade guidance; prioritize OpenAI docs MCP tools, use bundled references only as helper context, and restrict any fallback browsing to official OpenAI domains. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/skills/.system/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-creator: Create and scaffold plugin directories for Codex with a required `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, optional plugin folders/files, and baseline placeholders you can edit before publishing or testing. Use when Codex needs to create a new local plugin, add optional plugin structure, or generate or update repo-root `.agents/plugins/marketplace.json` entries for plugin ordering and availability metadata. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/skills/.system/plugin-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Guide for creating effective skills. This skill should be used when users want to create a new skill (or update an existing skill) that extends Codex's capabilities with specialized knowledge, workflows, or tool integrations. (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/skills/.system/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from another repo (including private repos). (file: C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.codex/skills/.system/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n### How to use skills\n- Discovery: The list above is the skills available in this session (name + description + file path). Skill bodies live on disk at the listed paths.\n- Trigger rules: If the user names a skill (with `$SkillName` or plain text) OR the task clearly matches a skill's description shown above, you must use that skill for that turn. Multiple mentions mean use them all. Do not carry skills across turns unless re-mentioned.\n- Missing/blocked: If a named skill isn't in the list or the path can't be read, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\n- How to use a skill (progressive disclosure):\n  1) After deciding to use a skill, open its `SKILL.md`. Read only enough to follow the workflow.\n  2) When `SKILL.md` references relative paths (e.g., `scripts/foo.py`), resolve them relative to the skill directory listed above first, and only consider other paths if needed.\n  3) If `SKILL.md` points to extra folders such as `references/`, load only the specific files needed for the request; don't bulk-load everything.\n  4) If `scripts/` exist, prefer running or patching them instead of retyping large code blocks.\n  5) If `assets/` or templates exist, reuse them instead of recreating from scratch.\n- Coordination and sequencing:\n  - If multiple skills apply, choose the minimal set that covers the request and state the order you'll use them.\n  - Announce which skill(s) you're using and why (one short line). If you skip an obvious skill, say why.\n- Context hygiene:\n  - Keep context small: summarize long sections instead of pasting them; only load extra files when needed.\n  - Avoid deep reference-chasing: prefer opening only files directly linked from `SKILL.md` unless you're blocked.\n  - When variants exist (frameworks, providers, domains), pick only the relevant reference file(s) and note that choice.\n- Safety and fallback: If a skill can't be applied cleanly (missing files, unclear instructions), state the issue, pick the next-best approach, and continue.\n</skills_instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<plugins_instructions>\n## Plugins\nA plugin is a local bundle of skills, MCP servers, and apps. Below is the list of plugins that are enabled and available in this session.\n### Available plugins\n- `Build iOS Apps`: Build iOS apps with workflows for App Intents, SwiftUI UI work, refactors, performance audits, and simulator debugging.\n- `Build Web Apps`: Build web apps with workflows for UI reviews, React improvements, deployment, payments, and database design.\n- `Canva`: Search, create, edit designs\n- `Figma`: Plugin that includes the Figma MCP server and Skills for common workflows.\n### How to use plugins\n- Discovery: The list above is the plugins available in this session.\n- Skill naming: If a plugin contributes skills, those skill entries are prefixed with `plugin_name:` in the Skills list.\n- Trigger rules: If the user explicitly names a plugin, prefer capabilities associated with that plugin for that turn.\n- Relationship to capabilities: Plugins are not invoked directly. Use their underlying skills, MCP tools, and app tools to help solve the task.\n- Preference: When a relevant plugin is available, prefer using capabilities associated with that plugin over standalone capabilities that provide similar functionality.\n- Missing/blocked: If the user requests a plugin that is not listed above, or the plugin does not have relevant callable capabilities for the task, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\n</plugins_instructions>"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"# AGENTS.md instructions for H:\\\n\n<INSTRUCTIONS>\n# Codex Global PRISM Rules\n\n## Spawned Agents In PRISM\n\nWhen working in `H:\\PRISM` and calling `spawn_agent`, treat spawned-agent awareness as mandatory.\n\nBefore every `spawn_agent` call in PRISM:\n\n1. use the `prism-spawn-awareness` skill\n2. run:\n\n```powershell\nnode C:\\Users\\Mark Villanueva\\.codex\\skills\\prism-spawn-awareness\\scripts\\emit_spawn_context.mjs --type \"<agent-type>\"\n```\n\n3. prepend the returned `additionalContext` to the spawned agent prompt\n4. prefer `fork_context: true` unless there is a specific reason not to\n\nThis keeps Codex spawned agents aligned with the same shared bundle Claude subagents receive through hooks:\n\n- current position and handoff\n- SVI / Psi / coverage alerts\n- shared coordination and workboard state\n- roadmap collaboration gate\n- MCP-development preference rules\n- search/index-first guidance\n- command-bridge awareness\n\nCanonical shared directive:\n\n- `H:/PRISM/state/shared/CLAUDE-CODEX-SPAWNED-AGENT-DIRECTIVE.md`\n\n## `/rgs-sync` In PRISM\n\nWhen the user types `/rgs-sync` while working in `H:\\PRISM`, mirror the shared roadmap-sync protocol instead of treating it as plain text.\n\n1. Read:\n   - `H:/PRISM/state/shared/CLAUDE-CODEX-RGS-SYNC-PROTOCOL.md`\n   - `H:/PRISM/state/shared/CLAUDE-CODEX-ROADMAP-EXECUTION-DIRECTIVE.md`\n   - `H:/PRISM/state/shared/ROADMAP_COLLABORATION_STATE.md`\n2. Run the canonical helper:\n\n```powershell\nnode H:\\PRISM\\.claude\\helpers\\roadmap-sync.mjs status\n```\n\n3. If the user supplies a structured sync payload, mirror it with:\n\n```powershell\nnode H:\\PRISM\\.claude\\helpers\\roadmap-sync.mjs sync --lane \"<lane>\" --status \"<status>\" --current \"<current>\" --next \"<next>\" --done \"<done>\" --blockers \"<blocker 1 || blocker 2>\" --needs \"<need 1 || need 2>\" --convergence-target \"<target>\"\n```\n\n4. Summarize the result in natural language, including:\n   - current collaboration mode\n   - whether the current backend/frontend finish-first gate is active\n   - whether a new large roadmap pass is allowed yet\n   - represented families/instances\n   - latest backend/frontend sync snapshots when available\n   - the most important sequencing reminder\n\n## Task Queue In PRISM\n\nWhen working in `H:\\PRISM`, treat the shared task queue as the canonical dependency-ordered assignment system.\n\nBefore planning new PRISM work in a fresh Codex session or after a reconnect:\n\n1. read:\n   - `H:/PRISM/state/shared/CLAUDE-CODEX-TASK-QUEUE-DIRECTIVE.md`\n   - `H:/PRISM/state/shared/TASK_COORDINATION_SPEC.md`\n   - `H:/PRISM/state/shared/TASK_QUEUE.md`\n2. run:\n\n```powershell\nnode H:\\PRISM\\.claude\\helpers\\task-queue.mjs reap\nnode H:\\PRISM\\.claude\\helpers\\task-queue.mjs next --agent-family Codex\n```\n\n3. if a Codex-owned task is available and matches the user's current request, prefer that task\n4. if no Codex task is available because dependencies are blocked, state the blocker clearly and continue only with non-conflicting frontend work\n5. when working a queue task, use:\n\n```powershell\nnode H:\\PRISM\\.claude\\helpers\\task-queue.mjs claim --task \"<id>\" --agent-family Codex\nnode H:\\PRISM\\.claude\\helpers\\task-queue.mjs start --task \"<id>\" --agent-family Codex\nnode H:\\PRISM\\.claude\\helpers\\task-queue.mjs heartbeat --task \"<id>\" --agent-family Codex\nnode H:\\PRISM\\.claude\\helpers\\task-queue.mjs complete --task \"<id>\" --agent-family Codex\n```\n\nThe task queue complements `/chat` and `/rgs-sync`:\n- use the queue for assignment/dependency truth\n- use `/chat` for short coordination notes\n- use `/rgs-sync` for roadmap convergence and sequencing\n\n## Command Bridge In PRISM\n\nWhen a PRISM slash command is referenced while working in `H:\\PRISM`, use the shared command bridge before treating it as informal plain text.\n\nRead:\n\n- `H:/PRISM/state/shared/CLAUDE-CODEX-COMMAND-BRIDGE.md`\n\nRule:\n\n- mirror markdown command specs by reading the indexed command file and following it as instructions\n- mirror hook pipelines by following the indexed helpers and settings behavior\n- do not invent alternate behavior when a canonical bridge/source already exists\n\n## `/startup` In PRISM\n\nWhen the user types `/startup` while working in `H:\\PRISM`, mirror the PRISM startup macro instead of treating it as a casual resume request.\n\nRead:\n\n- `C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.claude/commands/startup.md`\n- `H:/PRISM/state/shared/CLAUDE-CODEX-MCP-DEVELOPMENT-DIRECTIVE.md`\n- `H:/PRISM/state/shared/CLAUDE-CODEX-MCP-FULL-UTILIZATION-DIRECTIVE.md`\n- `H:/PRISM/state/shared/CLAUDE-CODEX-MCP-FULL-POWER-PLAYBOOK.md`\n- `H:/PRISM/state/shared/CLAUDE-CODEX-COMMAND-BRIDGE.md`\n\nThen prefer the PRISM startup stack for:\n\n- current position\n- handoff\n- coordination state\n- task queue\n- roadmap gate\n- build health\n- SVI / quality posture\n\nAlso mirror the shared startup heartbeat Claude is using:\n\n```powershell\nnode H:\\PRISM\\.claude\\helpers\\position-sync.mjs\nnode H:\\PRISM\\.claude\\helpers\\svi-refresh.mjs\nnode H:\\PRISM\\.claude\\helpers\\sync-memory.mjs\n```\n\nAnd after the session plan is known, post coordination state with:\n\n```powershell\nnode H:\\PRISM\\.claude\\helpers\\agent-coordination.mjs post --agent Codex --status ready --current \"<current>\" --next \"<next>\" --message \"<message>\"\nnode H:\\PRISM\\.claude\\helpers\\roadmap-sync.mjs sync --agent Codex --lane \"<lane>\" --status \"<status>\" --current \"<current>\" --next \"<next>\"\n```\n\n## Full Utilization Protocol In PRISM\n\nWhen doing meaningful PRISM work in `H:\\PRISM`, treat the full utilization directive as mandatory shared protocol, not optional reading.\n\nRead:\n\n- `H:/PRISM/state/shared/CLAUDE-CODEX-MCP-FULL-UTILIZATION-DIRECTIVE.md`\n\nAlways prefer it when deciding:\n\n- which `prism_dev` actions to use\n- which knowledge sources to consult\n- which helper scripts should fire at session start or before transition\n- which shared coordination writes should happen after a major work slice\n\n## `/smart` In PRISM\n\nWhen a PRISM task is explicitly invoked with `/smart`, or when the user expects the PRISM smart pipeline, mirror the command spec instead of doing silent ad hoc triage.\n\nRead:\n\n- `H:/PRISM/.claude/commands/smart.md`\n\nApply it as Codex operating behavior:\n\n- assess complexity\n- choose role posture\n- choose effort depth\n- state the configuration briefly when helpful\n- execute using the selected depth rather than generic default behavior\n\n## `/forge-triple` In PRISM\n\nWhen the user explicitly invokes `/forge-triple`, or when a PRISM task is clearly a net-new capability build that should compound the system, mirror the forge-triple pipeline instead of treating it as a normal isolated edit.\n\nRead:\n\n- `C:/Users/Mark Villanueva/.claude/commands/forge-triple.md`\n\nUse it as the canonical build discipline for capability work that should ship with:\n\n1. engine or core capability\n2. user-facing command / skill surface\n3. protective hook or enforcement surface\n\n## `/compact` In PRISM\n\nWhen the user references `/compact`, compaction recovery, or compact-aware resume behavior in `H:\\PRISM`, mirror the shared compact pipeline from the command bridge and helper stack.\n\nCanonical sources:\n\n- `H:/PRISM/state/shared/CLAUDE-CODEX-COMMAND-BRIDGE.md`\n- `H:/PRISM/.claude/helpers/pre-compact.mjs`\n- `H:/PRISM/.claude/helpers/compaction-survival.mjs`\n- `H:/PRISM/.claude/helpers/compact-restore.mjs`\n\n## MCP Development Surfaces In PRISM\n\nWhen working in `H:\\PRISM`, prefer the PRISM MCP-server development surfaces whenever they are the best shared source of truth instead of defaulting to ad hoc local shell behavior.\n\nCanonical directive:\n\n- `H:/PRISM/state/shared/CLAUDE-CODEX-MCP-DEVELOPMENT-DIRECTIVE.md`\n- `H:/PRISM/state/shared/CLAUDE-CODEX-MCP-FULL-UTILIZATION-DIRECTIVE.md`\n- `H:/PRISM/state/shared/CLAUDE-CODEX-MCP-FULL-POWER-PLAYBOOK.md`\n\nPrefer these surfaces when relevant:\n\n- `prism_dev:session_boot`\n- `prism_dev:build`\n- `prism_dev:server_info`\n- `prism_dev:test_smoke`\n- `prism_dev:test_results`\n- `prism_dev:svi_compute`\n- `prism_dev:svi_read`\n- `prism_dev:svi_summary`\n\nAnd the matching REST mirrors:\n\n- `/api/v1/dev/session-boot`\n- `/api/v1/dev/build`\n- `/api/v1/dev/server-info`\n- `/api/v1/dev/test/smoke`\n- `/api/v1/dev/test/results`\n- `/api/v1/dev/svi/compute`\n- `/api/v1/dev/svi/read`\n- `/api/v1/dev/svi/summary`\n</INSTRUCTIONS>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<environment_context>\n  <cwd>H:\\</cwd>\n 